WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.804
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.098 --> 00:00:11.041
Look at this chart.

00:00:11.041 --> 00:00:15.565
The figures show percent changes in
maternal mortality over a century long

00:00:15.565 --> 00:00:17.585
period, starting in 1784.

00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:21.696
And if you focus on AKH Vienna, you'll see
that there was actually a precipitous drop

00:00:21.696 --> 00:00:23.308
somewhere in the mid-century.

00:00:23.308 --> 00:00:27.309
When we isolate for several factors, we
discover that it probably had something to

00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:31.139
do with the discovery of the infectious
properties of medical equipment by Dr.

00:00:31.139 --> 00:00:33.110
Ignaz Semmelweis in the mid-century.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.615
This had a major effect
on women everywhere.

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:40.725
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That opening was just awful.

00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:45.080
[LAUGH] I started tuning Garett
out probably within the first few

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.290
seconds of his speech and
I'm guessing most of you did too.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.260
So, why are openings so important?

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.230
Well, it turns out, multiple studies have
shown that we have about ten seconds

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.120
to make a good first impression.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.039
That means if our first slide and
our first few sentences out of our

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:07.610
mouth don't capture our audience
attention, we've probably lost them.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.257
Now, is Akash Karia's book How to
Deliver a Great TED Talk, he suggests

00:01:13.257 --> 00:01:20.320
three techniques to open your speech
to capture your audience's attention.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.260
Now one technique is to open with a story.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.240
Melody Hobson started her TED talk
with a story about being mistaken for

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.690
a server at an event in
which she was a panelist.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.650
Our brains are wired for storytelling.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:42.230
When we hear a story, we create
a connection between us and the speaker.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.950
It makes that speech that
much more memorable for us.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.360
Another technique is
to begin with a quote.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.240
Elizabeth Pasany started
her Ted talk with a quote,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.420
people do stupid things,
that's what spreads HIV.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:03.670
The quote was short, relevant, provocative
but not one that's been overused.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:10.250
And it was impactful, because it was from
an authority on HIV, and it was her words.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.219
A third technique is to start
your talk with a question.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.580
Kelli McGonigal began her TED talk
with questions on how much stress

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.330
each person in the audience had
experienced in the past year.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:31.100
By asking questions and giving people
the time to think of a response,

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.880
she engaged her audience and
made her talk more relevant for them.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:41.070
Now, you don't need to utilize all of
these techniques in your speech, but

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.860
incorporating just one of
these elements into your talk

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.720
will help you capture your
audience's attention, and

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.100
make it more memorable, impactful,
and engaging from the start.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.250
So Garrett,
do you wanna give it another shot?

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:54.940
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:55.628
I'll try.

00:02:59.276 --> 00:03:05.380
Imagine it's 1849 and
you're lying in a hospital bed.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.620
It's actually a very uncomfortable bed,
more like a cart.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.460
And you look to your left and to your
right and you see a row of women who,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.760
like you, have been pregnant for
about nine months, and

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.500
you know that today is going to be
a very important day in your life.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.990
A nurse carts you into a smaller room,
it's very cramped, and you smell iodine.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.050
It's a pungent horrible odor.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.677
You hear cries and
screams from a hallway, and

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:35.955
you wonder as the doctor comes
in with giant forceps and

00:03:35.955 --> 00:03:41.070
this scary metal device,
is this going to be my last day on earth?

00:03:43.206 --> 00:03:45.730
Actually the question
is a very relevant one.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:51.150
If you're a woman in Vienna in 1849 and
you're expecting a child,

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:56.410
your chance of dying in the next
48 hours is one in five.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.580
That means that if there
are 50 women giving birth,

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.330
10 of them will be dead
within the next 48 hours.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.080
But that all changed in the next
year with the discovery

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.010
of the infectious properties
of medical equipment.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:14.730
That sharing medical equipment from
corpses to live women giving birth

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.660
caused infection, which killed mothers.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.717
This caused maternal mortality
rates to drop to one in 50.

00:04:21.717 --> 00:04:23.690
It was very significant.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.740
But this was all contingent on
the communication skills of

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.309
one doctor named Ignaz Semmelweis.

00:04:34.346 --> 00:04:39.750
Now I think that we can all agree
that that was a much better opening.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:43.970
Strong openings allow us to
connect with our audience, and

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.880
set the tone for
the rest of our presentation.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.850
Strong closings, however,
are no less important.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:55.295
Allow me to demonstrate
by finishing the story.

00:04:55.295 --> 00:05:02.900
Dr. Semmelweis was an early pioneer
of antiseptic practices in hospitals.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.140
However, he waited ten years
to publish his results.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:10.630
His results were rejected by the medical
community because they had no

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.070
basis in current theory.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.570
He castigated his colleagues,
accusing them of murder.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:20.950
And they, in turn,
conspired to have him committed

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:26.730
to a mental institution where he died
two weeks later of an infection.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:27.450
Thank you so much.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.810
It's been a pleasure presenting for
you today.

00:05:31.016 --> 00:05:32.771
&gt;&gt; Woah, woah, woah.

00:05:32.771 --> 00:05:35.463
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Wait a minute.

00:05:35.463 --> 00:05:40.220
Dr. Semmelweis' story may have
had an abrupt and bad ending,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.405
but that's no way to end a presentation.

00:05:43.405 --> 00:05:46.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Conclusions are just as

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.770
important as introductions.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:53.510
Oftentimes, we spend so much time
designing and preparing our beginnings but

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:56.040
then we rush through and
forget our endings.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:02.401
But, the last words out of your mouth
are gonna be what your audience remember

00:06:02.401 --> 00:06:06.188
when they walk out of the door, so
your conclusion should contain your

00:06:06.188 --> 00:06:09.673
central message, so
it's freshest in your listener's mind.

00:06:09.673 --> 00:06:10.443
In his book,

00:06:10.443 --> 00:06:15.350
Karia also gives us three techniques we
can use to end presentations effectively.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.900
Can anyone tell me where
this quote is from?

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.080
&gt;&gt; Abraham Lincoln?

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:24.690
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.600
This is the last line of
the Gettysburg Address.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.592
In this speech, which is now cited as one
of the most famous speeches in all of

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:36.648
history, Lincoln ended and
left his listeners with a message

00:06:36.648 --> 00:06:40.709
of hope as big and bright as
the civil war was dark and painful.

00:06:40.709 --> 00:06:46.207
200 years later,
we still remember this message of hope.

00:06:46.207 --> 00:06:50.061
The first pass that Karia gives

00:06:50.061 --> 00:06:54.959
us to end with hope for a better future.

00:06:54.959 --> 00:06:57.607
Second, sell the benefits.

00:06:57.607 --> 00:07:00.020
1,000 songs in your pocket.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.749
We all remember this
catchphrase from Steve Jobs,

00:07:02.749 --> 00:07:05.991
Apple product launch that we
watched earlier this quarter.

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:11.051
By repeating this line with
1,000 pop songs in your pocket,

00:07:11.051 --> 00:07:15.835
over and over, he made sure that
his audience knew the benefit

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:19.341
they would get if they
bought that iPod nano.

00:07:19.341 --> 00:07:21.240
And three, call to action.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:26.600
Ultimately, your conclusion should
have some sort of instructions for

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:29.640
your audience to take away and act on.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:35.950
Whether that's to learn CPR, to submit
your self-critique on time or to wash your

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:41.230
hands before surgery, your call to action
should be simple and easy to remember.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.260
&gt;&gt; Simple and easy to remember.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.285
That's not exactly how I would
describe the previous slide.

00:07:50.285 --> 00:07:56.548
[LAUGH]
When I say the words,

00:07:56.548 --> 00:07:59.970
business presentation,
what connotations come to mind?

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:07.210
Boring, difficult to remember,
maybe death by PowerPoint?

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.000
We can do much better than that.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.190
By using these simple techniques,

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:25.410
we can create beginnings that
compel our audiences and

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:29.680
endings that leave our audiences
with what we want them to remember.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.350
To me, the moral of the story
of Doctor Semmelweis is that

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.030
how we communicate really matters.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:41.680
He was right, but he failed to persuade.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.500
The way you communicate is important.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.761
Your life may not depend on it, but

00:08:51.761 --> 00:08:56.940
your effectiveness as
a business person surely does.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.869
[MUSIC]

